Routing for a page.jsonl page's children happens in two layers:

**1. Discovery at load time — `Page.fetch_listing(url)` (Page.class.js, via `Page.listing`/`Page.loaded_listing`)**

For a jsonl page already loaded, `Page.page_listing(url)` builds the listing straight from `page.listed` (the jsonl page's own in-memory map of file lines) — no fetch. That's the "my folder's own page.jsonl, IF something already loaded it" path described in `doc/declaring.md`'s moved comment (*was Page.class.js line 857*).

**2. Resolution at click/walk time — `Page.prototype.child(name, levels)` (Page.class.js)**

This is the actual router. For a jsonl page's child the relevant branch is:

```js
if (this.child_kinds?.get(name) === "jsonl") {
    const page = await Page.jsonl(this.url + name + "/");
    return page && this.add(name, page).load_all_children(levels);
}
```

`child_kinds` is populated by **Step 2 in `doc/jsonl.md`** — "a child page is LINKED, automatically": a line like `{"file": "kid/page.jsonl"}` or `{"file": "kid/page.js"}` calls `link_child(name)` (`Page.prototype.link_child`, delegating to `new this.constructor.Jsonl({page: this}).link_child(name)` in `Jsonl.js`), which records that `kid` is a jsonl child — keyed by the first path segment, so `kid/` and `kid/page.jsonl` are one entry and the latest line wins (per `doc/jsonl.md`'s "Three steps" section, point 2).

